I like the potion-above-the-door-trap idea, especially if the player can set
such traps on _open_ doors (kinda weird, but imagine that the door is only
open a bit; you can't prop a bucket above a closed door, right?).  That way,
you can pre-set a benificial potion trap on the way to doing battle with a
bunch of nasties.  On the way out, with monsters close behind, you spring
your own trap.

The trap gets sprung, and you get doused, in the same move that you cruise
through the doorway; usually it takes a move to slug down a potion.  Quite
often I find myself in just-one-more-move-and-i'da-lived situations.  By
Mark's trap-goodness metric, this hypothetical tactic should be considered
in future versions.

Of course, all sorts of things can go wrong with the trap: you drop the potion
on yourself while in the process of setting the trap; some other miscreant
springs the trap and gets speed, or healing, of whatever you'd set up; maybe
the potion doesn't fall on you but on the black dragon nibbling at your heels.

